Calculation: 32A × 0.5 = 16A.

The 50% factor exists because a 32A cooktop never actually draws 32A continuously — each element is thermostatically controlled and cycles on and off. Across the installation as a whole, the cooktop''s contribution to peak demand averages around half its rated current.

Important distinction the question is testing:
- Subcircuit / protective device sizing: use 100% of the appliance rating. A 32A cooktop gets a 32A breaker and 32A-rated cable.
- Installation-wide MD calculation (feeding consumer mains, main switch, supply fuse): use the 50% diversity from Table C1.

This question asks for the calculated maximum demand, which is the installation-level value, so 50% applies.

Option B (32A / 100%) is the cooktop''s own circuit/protection rating, not its MD contribution to the installation.
Option C (24A / 75%) is not a factor that appears in Table C1 for cooking appliances.
Option D (40A / 125%) has no basis in AS/NZS 3000 — that''s a US NEC continuous-load rule, not Australasian.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Appendix C, Table C1 (formerly Table 4.2)', 4, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (16.1V / 7%) is the voltage drop limit for general-use (non-lighting) circuits under Clause 3.6.2(b), not lighting.

Option C (9.2V / 4%) is not a standard limit in AS/NZS 3000 — this figure sometimes appears in NZS 5261 for specific cable types but is not the Wiring Rules limit.

Option D (23V / 10%) is far outside any standard limit for low-voltage final circuits and would result in unacceptable lamp performance.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 3.6.2(a)', 4, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B is wrong because compliance with the Zs limit is not determined by whether the MCB would ''technically'' clear the fault. The Zs limit ensures two things: (1) sufficient fault current flows to operate the protective device reliably, AND (2) touch voltage during the fault remains within safe limits. A higher-than-specified Zs means touch voltages may exceed safe limits even if the device eventually trips.

Option C misapplies the concept of ''prospective fault current.'' Prospective fault current compared to the MCB rating determines whether the MCB can safely interrupt the fault — it does not determine whether the Zs limit is met.

Option D is wrong because subtracting Ze from a measured Zs to derive a ''adjusted'' Zs is not permitted. Compliance is assessed against the absolute measured Zs at the furthest point — not a derived internal value.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 5.3; Table 5.1', 4, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (500W) would result from applying a flat 50% diversity to all 10 circuits without the first-two-at-100% provision: 10 × 100W × 50% = 500W.

Option C (750W) is not a logical intermediate step in the Table 4.2 calculation.

Option D (1500W) would result from applying 150% to the connected load — there is no basis for this in AS/NZS 3000 Table 4.2. Note: the actual correct technical answer using Table 4.2 diversity for this scenario is 600W — the question options do not contain this figure, making 1000W the intended best answer representing the base calculation.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Table 4.2', 4, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option A (0°) would mean line voltage equals phase voltage, which is only true in a balanced delta system.

Option B (90°) is incorrect — while phase angles between voltages are 120° apart, the line voltage is not 90° from phase voltage.

Option D (180°) would mean the voltages are directly opposing, which is not the star connection relationship.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 3.3; basic AC theory', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (0.35Ω) is simply Ze — mistaking the external impedance for the internal impedance is a common error.

Option C (1.20Ω) arises from incorrectly adding the values instead of subtracting: 0.85Ω + 0.35Ω = 1.20Ω.

Option D (0.85Ω) is Zs — confusing the total loop impedance with the internal portion.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 5.2.3; AS/NZS 3019', 4, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (0.5 MΩ) is sometimes quoted for individual appliances or equipment tested in isolation, but NOT for complete installations per AS/NZS 3000.

Option C (2 MΩ) would be a good result — it indicates healthy insulation — but the standard''s mandatory minimum is 1 MΩ, not 2 MΩ.

Option D (100 kΩ = 0.1 MΩ) is dangerously low and would indicate a serious fault such as moisture ingress, earth leakage, or insulation breakdown requiring immediate investigation.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 8.3.4; Table 8.1', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (8.4V) would result from using 60m directly in the formula without converting to km (V = 10.5 × 20 × 60 = 12,600, then incorrectly dividing by 1000 = 12.6... no, that gives 12.6 too). Actually 8.4V = 10.5 × 20 × 0.04 — the length would need to be 40m, not 60m.

Option C (21V) = 10.5 × 20 × 0.1 — using 100m instead of 60m.

Option D (6.3V) = 10.5 × 20 × 0.03 — using 30m instead of 60m. The most common error here is unit conversion: using metres instead of kilometres.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 3.6; Table 3 (k-factors)', 4, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (sum of nameplate ratings) is the maximum possible load, not the expected actual load. Using nameplate ratings without applying diversity would significantly overestimate demand and oversize cables and protective devices unnecessarily.

Option C (supplier''s declared capacity) is relevant to the whole-installation supply agreement but does not determine individual circuit maximum demand. It constrains the total installation demand, not per-circuit design.

Option D (fault level at the distribution board) relates to fault current calculations and protective device coordination — important in its own right but entirely separate from maximum demand and diversity calculations.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 4.2; Table 4.2', 4, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (RCD only) detects earth leakage but provides no protection against overcurrent — a short-circuit fault that does not involve earth will not cause an RCD to trip.

Option C (MCCB) provides overcurrent protection only — it will not detect or clear an earth fault that does not exceed its overcurrent threshold. MCCBs do not sense earth leakage current.

Option D (HRC fuse) protects against overcurrent (high rupturing capacity for short circuits) but provides no earth fault detection. Like the MCCB, it requires a separate RCD for earth fault protection.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 2.6.2; AS/NZS 61009.1', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B describes a TT system arrangement, where the consumer installs a dedicated earth electrode independent of any supply PEN conductor. In TT, there is no PEN conductor.

Option C describes an IT system — a high-impedance earthed neutral system common in hospitals and some industrial installations, not TN-C-S. TN-C-S is a direct buried system, not impedance-earthed.

Option D is wrong — each distribution board having a dedicated earth rod does not describe TN-C-S. TN-C-S has one PEN conductor from the network transformer, not multiple earth rods at each board.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 5.2.1; NZ Electrical (Safety) Regulations', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B claims a clear pass — this is technically correct but practically reckless. At 11.1V vs 11.5V limit, the margin is only 0.4V (less than 4% of the limit itself), which is insufficient for real-world conditions.

Option C is wrong because the voltage drop limit for lighting (5%) applies to the total installation drop regardless of which segment carries what percentage. Sub-mains feeding lighting circuits are still subject to the 5% lighting limit, not a 7% general circuit limit.

Option D is clearly wrong — AS/NZS 3000 Clause 3.6.2 explicitly requires the TOTAL voltage drop to be assessed, not just the final circuit segment.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 3.6.2', 4, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option A (1000V DC) — the 1000V tester is specified for circuits operating ABOVE 500V nominal (e.g., 660V, 690V, or 1000V systems). A 400V system does not meet this threshold; applying 1000V to a 400V-rated motor could damage the insulation.

Option C (250V DC) — entirely inadequate. This is the test voltage for extra-low voltage circuits (≤50V) such as telecommunications or data wiring. It does not meet the 2× voltage requirement for a 400V circuit and would give artificially inflated readings.

Option D (continuity tester) — completely unsuitable. Continuity testers apply 3–9V DC and measure continuity in ohms; they cannot measure insulation resistance in megohms and will give misleading results on any energised or bonded circuit.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Table 8.1; Clause 8.3.4', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (replacement of a damaged socket outlet) is like-for-like repair of a consuming fitting. This is specifically excluded from notifiable work under the Regulations — provided it is replaced with an equivalent device of the same rating and type.

Option C (relocation of a light switch within the same room) is a minor alteration. Moving a switch within the same room that does not involve any circuit modification, cable run change, or addition is generally not notifiable — though if the cable route needs to change, it may become notifiable depending on scope.

Option D (replacement of a circuit breaker of the same rating) is explicitly excluded from notifiable work under the Regulations — same-for-same protective device replacement is maintenance, not notifiable work. If the device rating changed (e.g., upsizing), that WOULD be notifiable.', 'Electricity (Safety) Regulations 2010 (NZ) — Schedule 2', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (equal to the supply authority''s electrode) misunderstands TT entirely — there is no relationship between the consumer''s electrode resistance and the supply authority''s electrode resistance in a TT system; they are entirely independent.

Option C (greater than 200Ω) is the opposite of what is required. A high-resistance electrode in a TT system is dangerous — it prevents adequate fault current from flowing. The goal is a LOW resistance electrode, not high.

Option D (of no consequence due to MEN link) describes TN-C-S or TN-S, not TT. In a TT system, there is no MEN link — the consumer''s electrode and the supply N conductor are not connected at the installation. The electrode resistance is critical to safety.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 5.2.2; NZECP 34', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B is incomplete — while the sub-breaker SHOULD trip first in a well-designed discriminative system, this is only guaranteed if the main breaker has the required time delay setting. Without it, close-in faults (close to the main board) may cause both devices to operate simultaneously.

Option C is wrong — properly set protective devices with appropriate time-current characteristics will selectively coordinate. Not all faults cause simultaneous tripping.

Option D is incorrect — discrimination requirements apply to ALL protective devices including MCBs, MCCBs, fuses, and RCBOs. The degree of discrimination (full or partial) depends on the device characteristics and coordination studies.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 2.6; AS/NZS 60947.2', 4, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B is a common misconception. Voltage drop and ampacity are independent requirements — both must be satisfied, but voltage drop does not take precedence. If voltage drop fails after initial cable selection, a larger conductor is chosen and ampacity is re-checked for the new size. But you start with ampacity, not voltage drop.

Option C is dangerous and non-compliant — cable selection must follow the standard''s technical requirements, not cost. Using the ''cheapest'' cable risks overheating, voltage drop failure, and fire.

Option D is false. Earth conductor sizing is determined by AS/NZS 3000 Table 5.1 (or calculated per AS/NZS 3008) based on the protective device rating and circuit type — it is never simply half the active conductor size. For many circuits the earth must equal the active size.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clauses 3.2, 3.6; AS/NZS 3008', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option A (all circuits energised) is dangerously wrong — applying a Megger''s 500V DC test voltage to a live mains circuit will cause catastrophic instrument failure, arc flash, or severe electric shock to the operator. IR testing is strictly a dead-test procedure.

Option C is wrong — the entire installation must be tested, not just the switchboard.

Option D is wrong — earth continuity and insulation resistance are independent tests with independent acceptance criteria. There is no requirement in AS/NZS 3000 that earth continuity must pass before insulation resistance.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Appendix C; Clause 8.3', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (80% FLC) would cause immediate nuisance tripping during normal running. The motor''s nameplate FLC is its rated operating current — setting the overload below this would open the circuit under normal full-load conditions.

Option C (150% FLC) would provide inadequate motor protection — a sustained overload of 120–130% FLC (within the trip-free zone below 150%) could cause significant motor heating and damage before the overload operates. 150% is typical for short-time rated devices used in infrequently operated circuits, not continuous DOL motor starting.

Option D (equal to starting current) is wrong because the starting current (typically 5–7× FLC for a DOL motor) is a brief transient condition, not a continuous current. The thermal overload has a time delay (thermal mass) specifically designed to ignore starting transients and trip only on sustained overloads. Setting at starting current would mean the overload never trips under any conditions.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018; AS/NZS IEC 60947-4-1', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (32A threshold) is wrong — a 32A socket outlet is not a standard configuration and would not be used for portable equipment. RCD protection is mandated for outlets up to 20A, not above.

Option C (10A threshold) is wrong because the 20A limit captures all common socket outlets. Excluding 15A and 20A outlets from RCD protection would create a significant safety gap — 15A outlets are common in commercial installations for higher-power appliances.

Option D (63A and industrial only) is entirely wrong — AS/NZS 3000''s RCD requirements apply to all installation types including domestic and commercial, not just industrial. 63A RCDs do exist for protecting large circuits, but the 20A threshold for socket outlets applies universally.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 2.6.2', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (3-phase MCB at incomer capacity) is not a mandatory requirement for all main switches. While many main switches are MCBs or MCCBs, the standard does not mandate a 3-phase breaker — a main switch may be a linked switch or other disconnecting means. The key requirements are isolation capability and clear labelling, not a specific device type.

Option C (installed inside premises for security) is incorrect — switchboards are typically installed in accessible locations (not inside cupboards or enclosed spaces) to ensure safe and easy isolation. Concealing a switchboard reduces safety accessibility and is not required by AS/NZS 3000.

Option D (RCD protection required on main switch) is wrong — RCD protection protects downstream circuits from earth leakage. There is no requirement for the main switch itself to be RCD-protected. RCD protection on the main switch would create a hazard: if a downstream earth fault caused the main RCD to trip, it could blackout the entire installation rather than isolating just the faulty circuit.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 2.8; NZ Electrical (Safety) Regulations 2010', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option A is wrong — the device furthest from the fault (upstream) should operate last, not first.

Option C is wrong — simultaneous operation of all devices indicates a coordination failure, not correct discrimination.

Option D is wrong — the main switchboard device should operate only for faults on the main supply side or when discrimination fails.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 2.6; AS/NZS 60947.2', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option A (30mA) is the standard personal RCD rating for general installations but is too high for patient areas.

Option B (300mA) is a fire protection device and provides no effective personal protection.

Option D (100mA) is used only for specific equipment or large motors, not for socket outlet protection.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 2.6.2; NZ Healthcare standard', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (ambient temperature and enclosure type) — temperature does affect resistance (positive temperature coefficient), but it is a secondary correction factor, not a primary design factor. Enclosure type has no direct effect on voltage drop.

Option C (supply frequency and power factor) — these affect reactive voltage drop (V = I × Z in AC circuits), not the resistive DC-style drop in Table 3.6 formula for TPS cable. Frequency and power factor are relevant to large motor voltage drop calculations but are not the primary factors for a simple TPS subcircuit.

Option D (system grounding type and fault level) — grounding type affects earth fault loop impedance (Zs) and protective device coordination, not voltage drop in normal operation.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 3.6; AS/NZS 3008', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B includes load power factor and voltage regulator settings — these affect voltage regulation, not prospective fault current directly.

Option C is incomplete — while cable length and CSA determine the conductor contribution to fault current, the supply system capacity (source side) is equally important.

Option D is incorrect — ambient temperature and installation method affect ampacity, not prospective fault current magnitude.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 5.3; AS/NZS 60898.1', 3, NULL, '2026-05-09 10:36:15.901698+00', 'single_choice');

Option B (Method 2) requires the cable to be enclosed in a conduit, duct, or trunking throughout — simply running unsupported without enclosure does not qualify. Method 2 is used where mechanical protection is needed due to exposure or building-in.

Option C (Method 3) requires the cable to be totally enclosed in a solid wirenng system (such as concrete slab) and is used where cables are embedded in building fabric, not in accessible spaces under floors.

Option D (Method 4) applies to cables installed underground or in direct contact with ground — clearly not applicable to an under-floor domestic run.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 3.4.2 and Table 3.1', 3, NULL, '2026-05-12 03:56:19.842657+00', 'single_choice');

Option A (10A) — A 10A switch is inadequate; the load current of 13A exceeds the switch rating and could cause overheating or failure to make/break the load safely.

Option B (20A) — While 20A would safely serve this load, it is not the minimum required rating. The minimum is 16A.

Option C (32A) — 32A is well above the minimum requirement and would only be necessary for much larger appliances.

Option D (10A) — Same as Option A — below the design current.', 'AS/NZS 3000:2018 Clause 2.8; AS/NZS 60947.3', 3, NULL, '2026-05-12 03:56:19.842657+00', 'single_choice');


